// PARAM: --analysis interval
// Adapted from Goblint tests/regression/34-localwn_restart/03-nested2.c
// (Amato-Scozzari, SAS 2013): an endless outer loop whose body runs a
// counted inner loop and then steps i := i + 11 - j.
//
// Goblint expects i >= 0 there, but only under
// sem.int.signed_overflow=assume_none. VIMP freezes the wraparound policy
// instead, and under it the property is simply false: the outer loop never
// terminates, i grows by one per iteration, and after 2^31 iterations
// i + 1 wraps to int32's minimum and the check fails. Both a satisfying and
// a violating execution exist, so UNKNOWN is the only sound answer and any
// verdict here would be wrong rather than imprecise.
//
// What the fixture still exercises is the narrowing the original example is
// about: the inner head widens j to [0,+inf] and narrows it back to [0,10],
// leaving j = [10,10] on the exit edge, so the outer step is i + 1 rather
// than i + [1,11]. Losing that would leave i unconstrained for a second,
// unrelated reason.
void main() {
  int32 i, j;
  i := 0;
  while (true) {
    j := 0;
    while (j < 10) {
      j := j + 1
    };
    i := i + 11 - j;
    __voblint_check(!(i < 0)) // UNKNOWN
  }
}
